- Wired to cron `no_agent` script mode → pushes to WeChat. Omi's existing stock crons: hourly monitor, 9:20 DSA morning, 14:55 pre-close, 18:10 daily report. Add signal cron (e.g. 15:30 close) to complement, don't spam.
- Respect Omi's rule: don't auto-run report crons as a reply to every message — only run on explicit "跑一次".
- **On-demand re-push**: to send a fresh report now, run the cron via `cronjob action=run job_id=<id>` (keeps `deliver: weixin`); verify `last_status: ok` in the return.

### Current-position-aware signal (make every push reflect Omi's real book)
- `signal_daily.py` hardcodes `HOLDINGS = {code: (shares, cost)}` and `CASH`. Report opens with a 【当前持仓】 block (市值, 浮盈, per-position signal) then the 【自选股信号】 list, and marks held tickers `🟢持仓`.
- **CRITICAL workflow**: after every real trade, UPDATE `HOLDINGS` and `CASH` in the script — otherwise daily pushes silently report stale positions and wrong 总资产. Tell Omi this each time you update it.
- **Obsidian master file is the human-readable record (2026-09-01)**: Omi's positions, 交易历史, and 方案A live in `/Users/omi/Documents/macos hermes/04-Trading/Omi A股交易主文件.md`. Every real trade must update THREE places in sync: (1) the Obsidian master file (持仓 + 交易历史 tables), (2) `signal_daily.py` HOLDINGS/CASH, (3) `stop-loss-watch.py` STOP_LOSS (add held ticker, remove on exit) + its cron name. Then `git add -A && git commit` the vault. Obsidian = human-readable truth, scripts = executable truth — never let them drift. MEMORY keeps only a one-line pointer to the master file (memory char ceiling). Full recipe incl. memory-overflow relief + Obsidian-vs-local decision: `references/obsidian-master-file-sync.md`.
- **Pitfall — Sina date type (single source)**: Sina source returns `datetime.date`, not pandas `Timestamp`, so `last["date"].date()` crashes with `'datetime.date' object has no attribute 'date'`. Guard with `d = last["date"]; date_str = str(d.date() if hasattr(d, "date") else d)`.
- **Pitfall — mixed date types after concat (the subtle one)**: Eastmoney returns `datetime.date`, Sina returns `Timestamp`. When per-year frames from BOTH sources are `pd.concat`'d (normal when a year fails on Eastmoney and falls back to Sina), the `date` column holds BOTH types → `df.sort_values("date")` throws `Cannot compare Timestamp with datetime.date`. Surfaces in the live signal as "数据失败: Cannot compare Timestamp with datetime.date" for every ticker, even though `fetch_history` alone looks fine. **FIX (root cause, one line)**: after `pd.concat(frames)`, add `df["date"] = pd.to_datetime(df["date"])` to force one dtype. **CRITICAL ordering: the `to_datetime` MUST run BEFORE `drop_duplicates(subset=["date"]).sort_values("date")`** — if it's placed after the sort, `sort_values("date")` still crashes on mixed frames. Verified 2026-08-26: `signal_daily.py 601899` failed with this exact error until the normalization was moved above the sort/dedup line. Debug note: when "数据失败" won't reproduce in isolation, run the exact report path (`signal_daily.py`) — direct `compute_signal` calls can hide the mixed-frame bug the concat path triggers.
- **Pitfall — slow multi-fetch**: holdings block + signal block used to fetch the same stock twice. Cache results in a dict keyed by code and reuse (store the Exception, re-raise on use) to halve network calls. Without this, a 5-ticker run can exceed 180s.
- **Pitfall — intraday run = T-1 (stale) prices (user-caught 2026-08-14)**: `fetch_history()` pulls **daily K-line history** and reports the last *closed* bar. If the signal cron fires **during the trading session** (e.g. 15:35), today's daily bar isn't closed yet, so every ticker shows **yesterday's close** — not the live price. Omi caught this when the report showed 亨通光电 at 57.25 while it had already limit-up'd +10% to 62.98 intraday. **FIX**: schedule the daily-signal cron to run **after market close** (e.g. 18:35, also avoiding the 18:10 daily-report push for WeChat rate-limit reasons). If the user wants a true intraday price, use the Tencent realtime quote endpoint (`qt.gtimg.cn/q=<code>` with `Referer: http://finance.qq.com`) instead of the daily-K fetch. Rule of thumb: daily-K-based signals are *close-of-day recaps*, never intraday prices — say so when scheduling them.

